BEGIN;
DO $$
DECLARE
  previous jsonb;
  additions jsonb := '[{"id":"exam-readiness","title":"CFP® exam readiness plan","html":"<h3>CFP® exam readiness plan</h3><p>The Academy’s 70-question graded review checks the concepts from the lessons. Then use the eight fresh applied case drills to practice making a decision, doing the calculations and explaining what would change the answer. Check your reasoning before reviewing the worked solution.</p><ol><li>Study all seven courses. Complete the capstone and correct any mistakes in assumptions, calculations, client interests or scope.</li><li>Set a 20-minute timer and work the eight case questions in the Exam application drill. Treat blank and lucky answers as review items. Complete the follow-up calculations and written reasoning afterward.</li><li>Take a fresh official CFP Board full-length practice exam under test conditions. Use the approved calculator, match the two three-hour sessions and work with the tax tables for your exam window. Complete the first run with notes closed.</li><li>Log every miss and guess by domain, cause and correction. Prioritize weaknesses by official weighting and test them with fresh questions. Explain the rule and two wrong choices without referring to the answer.</li><li>Take a second fresh full-length practice exam after remediation. Look for consistent performance across domains, accurate assumptions and enough time to complete both sessions. Practice results guide study and CFP Board determines the actual exam result.</li></ol><h4>Use your error log</h4><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Approximate exam weight</th><th>Track errors in</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ethics and regulation</td><td>8%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr><tr><td>General principles</td><td>15%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr><tr><td>Insurance planning</td><td>11%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr><tr><td>Investment planning</td><td>17%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr><tr><td>Tax planning</td><td>14%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr><tr><td>Retirement income</td><td>18%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr><tr><td>Estate planning</td><td>10%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr><tr><td>Client psychology</td><td>7%</td><td>Knowledge · computation · judgment · pacing</td></tr></tbody></table><h4>Official resources</h4><ul><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.cfp.net/certification-process/exam-requirement/cfp-exam-preparation/exam-prep-resources/cfp-practice-exam\">Official full-length practice exams</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.cfp.net/certification-process/exam-requirement/about-the-cfp-exam/exam-format\">Exam format and pacing</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.cfp.net/certification-process/exam-requirement/cfp-exam-preparation/exam-prep-resources/tax-tables-and-formulas\">Exam-window tax tables and formulas</a></li><li><a target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\" href=\"https://www.cfp.net/certification-process/exam-requirement/about-the-cfp-exam/scoring-and-results\">Official scoring and results</a></li></ul>"},{"id":"exam-application","title":"Timed exam application drill","html":"<h3>Timed exam application drill</h3><p>Allow 20 minutes. Write down all eight answers before opening the worked solutions. Give yourself one point per correct selection. Separately mark whether you can explain your reasoning. Use the extension prompts as untimed practice and discuss your reasoning with a mentor.</p><h4>1. Ethics and regulation</h4><p>A planner can retain a client’s assets in a low-cost employer plan or move them to an IRA that pays the planner an advisory fee. The client is 56, left the employer this year and may need $25,000 next year. No plan statements or distribution rules have been provided.</p><p><strong>What is the best immediate action?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Recommend the employer plan solely because its fee is lower.</li><li>Tell the client the IRA necessarily preserves every plan benefit.</li><li>Gather plan and IRA terms, clarify the withdrawal need and disclose the compensation conflict before the recommendation.</li><li>Transfer now and compare later.</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>C. Gather plan and IRA terms, clarify the withdrawal need and disclose the compensation conflict before the recommendation.</p><p>A defensible recommendation rests on client circumstances and comparable alternatives. Liquidity, any age-55 separation exception, investment options, costs and creditor protections can matter. Verify plan terms and tax eligibility. Disclose the material conflict, obtain informed consent and manage it.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> List two facts that could reverse your recommendation and draft a four-sentence memo that documents the client’s options.</p><h4>2. General principles</h4><p>A client has $26,000 in cash, spends $4,800 monthly on essentials and pays $600 in required debt service outside that figure. A tuition bill of $30,000 is due in five years. Assume a hypothetical 4% annual discount rate.</p><p><strong>How much must be set aside today for tuition, ignoring taxes and fees?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$30,000</li><li>$36,500</li><li>About $25,960</li><li>About $24,658</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>D. About $24,658</p><p>Divide $30,000 by 1.04⁵. If debt minimums are essential, six months of essential spending is $32,400, leaving a $6,400 reserve shortfall before using cash for education. Do not use the same dollars for both goals.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> Recalculate the tuition deposit at 2%. Explain how you would present the funding trade-off to the client.</p><h4>3. Insurance planning</h4><p>A household needs $520,000 for survivor income, $250,000 for debt and $50,000 for transition costs. It has $85,000 dedicated assets and $150,000 employer life. One spouse expects to leave that employer before retirement.</p><p><strong>What individual gap would you initially model if group coverage disappears?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$670,000</li><li>$735,000</li><li>$585,000</li><li>$820,000</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>B. $735,000</p><p>Total need is $820,000. Subtract $85,000 of dedicated assets, yielding $735,000 without group coverage. With group coverage the gap is $585,000. Compare conversion rights, portability, term and insurability.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> Show how an additional $100,000 in dedicated assets affects both cases. Explain why insuring only the amount of the mortgage misses the income need.</p><h4>4. Investment planning</h4><p>A $500,000 portfolio targets 60% equities and 40% fixed income. Holdings are $355,000 equities and $145,000 bonds. The stock adjustment can be made either in a traditional IRA or by selling taxable shares with unrealized gains. The bond sleeve has modified duration 6.</p><p><strong>What action best restores the mix without realizing a taxable gain now, assuming sufficient IRA holdings and no distribution?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Shift $55,000 from equities to bonds inside the IRA.</li><li>Shift $55,000 from bonds to equities in taxable.</li><li>Shift $105,000 from equities to bonds in taxable.</li><li>Withdraw $55,000 from the IRA and buy bonds in taxable.</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>A. Shift $55,000 from equities to bonds inside the IRA.</p><p>Target equities are $300,000. An internal trade in the IRA does not itself distribute assets. A 0.75 percentage point yield rise implies roughly a 4.5% bond price decline before convexity.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> Estimate the rate-shock dollar loss on the $200,000 target bond allocation. Explain how the answer changes if the client needs cash next year.</p><h4>5. Tax planning</h4><p>A married couple has $70,000 in taxable income before a proposed Roth conversion. Use an illustrative schedule: ordinary income through $100,000 is taxed at 12% for incremental dollars in the exercise and the next bracket at 22%. Ignore all other interactions for the first pass.</p><p><strong>What is the incremental federal tax on a $40,000 conversion under these assumptions?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$8,800</li><li>$3,600</li><li>$5,800</li><li>$4,800</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>C. $5,800</p><p>The first $30,000 uses the illustrative 12% bracket and the next $10,000 uses 22%, giving $3,600 plus $2,200. For actual analysis check income, withholding, credits, taxable basis, Medicare premiums and future marginal rates.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> Compare incremental tax for a $30,000 versus $40,000 conversion. Identify one reason the client might choose the smaller conversion.</p><h4>6. Retirement income</h4><p>A retiring couple spends $84,000 yearly including tax and has $48,000 in reliable income. Their portfolio is $900,000. A 15% decline occurs before the first withdrawal. Ignore inflation for year one.</p><p><strong>What is the required first withdrawal as a percentage of the reduced portfolio?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>About 4.71%</li><li>4.00%</li><li>About 5.23%</li><li>9.33%</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>A. About 4.71%</p><p>The portfolio gap is $36,000. The decline reduces assets to $765,000. Divide $36,000 by $765,000. A higher draw percentage after an early loss increases pressure on future funding. Model flexible spending and cash reserves.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> If the couple cuts discretionary spending by $6,000, recompute the first draw rate. Explain how to model survivor income when one spouse dies.</p><h4>7. Estate planning</h4><p>A household expects $140,000 in estate expenses and short-term support needs. Estate-accessible cash is $60,000. A $300,000 life policy names an adult child and a $200,000 brokerage account is individually titled without a transfer-on-death designation. Assume no immediate access to the brokerage account pending administration.</p><p><strong>What immediate liquidity gap should the advisor model?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>$0 because of life insurance alone</li><li>$140,000</li><li>$220,000</li><li>$80,000</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>D. $80,000</p><p>Only $60,000 is specified as immediately available to the estate. Policy proceeds are payable according to the beneficiary designation. Counsel should verify titling, access, authority and whether lawful borrowing or other funding could bridge the gap.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> Sketch a table of legal owner, designated recipient, estimated timing and access for each asset. Explain why estate tax inclusion and probate transfer can differ.</p><h4>8. Client psychology</h4><p>A client with adequate time and reserves asks to liquidate equities after a sharp decline. In earlier interviews the client said that another 20% decline would be emotionally difficult. No change in spending needs has been disclosed.</p><p><strong>Which response best starts the next conversation?</strong></p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Trade immediately without clarifying which assets or goals are affected.</li><li>Acknowledge the concern and verify what changed. Review the agreed risks, alternatives and likely consequences together.</li><li>Tell the client their feelings are irrelevant.</li><li>Guarantee that the market will recover soon.</li></ol><details><summary>Reveal worked solution</summary><p>B. Acknowledge the concern and verify what changed. Review the agreed risks, alternatives and likely consequences together.</p><p>The planner needs to distinguish a durable change in tolerance or capacity from a reaction to recent losses. Explain trade-offs in plain language and verify the client’s decision and lawful instructions.</p></details><p><strong>Practice extension</strong> Draft a teach-back prompt and state one condition that would justify changing the investment policy.</p>"}]'::jsonb;
BEGIN
  SELECT lessons INTO STRICT previous FROM public.tac_academy_courses
  WHERE slug = 'financial-planning-cfp-prep' FOR UPDATE;
  IF jsonb_array_length(previous) < 3 OR previous->0->>'id' <> 'start' OR previous->2->>'id' <> 'video' THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'CFP prep lesson order has changed';
  END IF;
  IF NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM jsonb_array_elements(previous) l WHERE l->>'id' IN ('exam-readiness','exam-application')) THEN
    UPDATE public.tac_academy_courses SET lessons = jsonb_insert(previous, '{3}', additions->0)
      WHERE slug = 'financial-planning-cfp-prep';
    UPDATE public.tac_academy_courses SET lessons = jsonb_insert(lessons, '{4}', additions->1)
      WHERE slug = 'financial-planning-cfp-prep';
  END IF;
END;
$$;
COMMIT;
